#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204011 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83df73fc-c0cc-5605-9b39-453de0c37cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating S(x,y) Using the Shortcut Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "dfeb687f-763d-54c4-aef3-8444a3902349" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202606}] :question/id #uuid "d5e13c72-a4ef-5310-a416-6c1b8d1065c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-251491" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $25$ paired observations $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{25}$, and $y_{1}, y_{2}, …, y_{25}$. You are given that\n$∑_(i = 1)^(25)x_{i} = 250,; ∑_(i = 1)^(25)y_{i} = 265,; ∑_(i = 1)^(25)x_{i}y_{i} = 3625$.Then, $S_{xy} =$\n{{field-1}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f0ed04b-f011-5818-952d-425a19e0cd5c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums of Squares Using the Shortcut Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e22bc458-30f2-56c5-a752-2720115543c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22bc458-30f2-56c5-a752-2720115543c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536636} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae99c74-d6dc-56f1-8e0e-a0b4df319323" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cbb4944-93ba-5c18-a003-1720623144e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-251837" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $27$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{27},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{27}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{27}{x}_{i}=540,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{27}{y}_{i}=567, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{27}{x}_{i}^{2}=12\\,050,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{27}{y}_{i}^{2}=14\\,107.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the sum of the squared deviations from the mean for the points ${x}_{i}.$\n\n${S}_{xx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & =12\\,050-\\frac{1}{27}(540)^{2} \\\\  & =12\\,050-10\\,800 \\\\  & =1\\,250.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
